\paragraph{Market shares [2.5 points]} Assume that stratified random sampling was used to produce your sample. The sample was divided into strata (i.e., groups) and simple random sampling was used to sample observations in each stratum. We consider the following strata:

\begin{itemize}
	\item \textbf{S1:} females aged 40 years or younger;
	\item \textbf{S2:} females aged 41 years or older;
	\item \textbf{S3:} males aged 40 years or younger;
	\item \textbf{S4:} males aged 41 years or older.
\end{itemize}

Table~\ref{tab:table1} gives the size of each category in the full population.

\begin{table}[h]
	\centering
	\begin{tabular}{c|cc}
		       & $\text{Age} \leq 40$ &  $\text{Age} > 40$ \\
		\hline
		  Male &            2'676'249 &           1'633'263\\
		Female &            2'599'058 &           1'765'143
	\end{tabular}
	\captionof{table}{London population estimates in 2015 (Source: ONS)\label{tab:table1}}
\end{table}

\begin{enumerate}
	\item Report the size and weight of each stratum in your sample \textbf{[1~point]}.
\end{enumerate}

{\itshape
% % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % %
All market shares are computed with our preferred model, Model 3 ($\text{Model}_\text{pref}$), with the logarithm of travel times, the interaction of age with the ASCs and the traffic variability for car. The total population is $N = 2'599'058 + 1'765'143 + 2'676'249 + 1'633'263 = 8'673'713$ and the sample size is $S = 5'000$. Each observation $n$ belonging to stratum $g$ receives the weight:
\[
\omega_g = \frac{N_g / N}{S_g / S}
\]
where $N_g$ is the size of stratum $g$ in the population and $S_g$ its size in the sample. A weight greater than 1 means that the stratum is under-represented in the sample, and a weight lower than 1 means that it is over-represented. As a check, the sum of the weights over the whole sample is equal to $\sum_{n} \omega_{g(n)} = 5'000 = S$.

\begin{table}[h]
	\centering
	\begin{tabular}{l|cccc|c}
		Stratum & $N_g$ & $N_g/N$ & $S_g$ & $S_g/S$ & Weight $\omega_g$ \\
		\hline
		S1: Female, $\text{Age} \leq 40$ & 2'599'058 & 0.2996 & 1'415 & 0.2830 & 1.0588 \\
		S2: Female, $\text{Age} > 40$    & 1'765'143 & 0.2035 & 1'195 & 0.2390 & 0.8515 \\
		S3: Male, $\text{Age} \leq 40$   & 2'676'249 & 0.3085 & 1'251 & 0.2502 & 1.2332 \\
		S4: Male, $\text{Age} > 40$      & 1'633'263 & 0.1883 & 1'139 & 0.2278 & 0.8266 \\
		\hline
		Total & 8'673'713 & 1 & 5'000 & 1 & -- \\
	\end{tabular}
	\captionof{table}{Size and weight of each stratum in the sample\label{tab:weights}}
\end{table}

The young individuals (S1 and S3), and especially the young males (S3, $\omega = 1.23$), are under-represented in our sample, while the individuals aged 41 or older (S2 and S4) are over-represented.
% % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % %
