\begin{enumerate}[resume]
	\item Using $\text{Model}_\text{pref}$ and the weights of your strata, compute the predicted market share of each mode and their confidence intervals. Do the obtained results match your expectations? \textbf{[1~point]}
\end{enumerate}

{\itshape
% % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % %
Using $\text{Model}_\text{pref}$, we first simulate for each individual $n$ the probability $P_n(i)$ of choosing each alternative $i$. The predicted market share of alternative $i$ is then the weighted mean of these individual probabilities:
\[
W(i) = \frac{1}{S} \sum_{n=1}^{S} \omega_{g(n)} \cdot P_n(i)
\]
To obtain the confidence intervals, we re-estimate $\text{Model}_\text{pref}$ with bootstrapping (100 draws). This gives a sample of 100 values of the parameters, which we use to simulate the probabilities and compute the 90\% confidence interval of each market share.

\begin{table}[h]
	\centering
	\begin{tabular}{l|cc}
		Mode & Predicted market share & 90\% confidence interval \\
		\hline
		Walking          & 18.8\% & [17.6\% ; 19.9\%] \\
		Cycling          & 2.9\%  & [2.4\% ; 3.5\%] \\
		Public transport & 35.1\% & [33.4\% ; 36.8\%] \\
		Car              & 43.2\% & [41.2\% ; 45.4\%] \\
	\end{tabular}
	\captionof{table}{Weighted market shares predicted by $\text{Model}_\text{pref}$\label{tab:ms}}
\end{table}

The results match our expectations:
\begin{itemize}
	\item \textbf{Car} is the most chosen mode (43.2\%), followed by \textbf{public transport} (35.1\%). This is consistent with the London metropolitan area, which has a very dense public transport network, but where a large part of the trips are made in the outer areas where the car stays very competitive.
	\item \textbf{Walking} has a significant share (18.8\%). It is the preferred mode for short trips, which is consistent with the strong negative effect of the logarithm of walking time ($\beta_{time\_walk} = -4.19$): walking is very attractive for short trips but quickly loses its utility when the trip gets longer.
	\item \textbf{Cycling} has a very low share (2.9\%), which is consistent with the very negative $ASC_{cycle}$ ($-2.58$) and with the known low cycling share in London.
\end{itemize}
% % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % %
\par}\medskip

\begin{enumerate}[resume]
	\item Compare the market shares predicted by $\text{Model}_\text{pref}$ with the weighted market shares computed using the actual choices. \textbf{[0.5~point]}
\end{enumerate}

{\itshape
% % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % %
The weighted market shares of the actual choices are computed with the same weights, by replacing the predicted probabilities with the observed choices:
\[
W_{obs}(i) = \frac{1}{S} \sum_{n=1}^{S} \omega_{g(n)} \cdot y_{in}
\qquad \text{with } y_{in} = 1 \text{ if individual } n \text{ chose } i, \text{ and } 0 \text{ otherwise.}
\]

\begin{table}[h]
	\centering
	\begin{tabular}{l|ccc}
		Mode & Predicted ($\text{Model}_\text{pref}$) & 90\% CI & Actual (weighted) \\
		\hline
		Walking          & 18.8\% & [17.6\% ; 19.9\%] & 18.9\% \\
		Cycling          & 2.9\%  & [2.4\% ; 3.5\%]   & 3.0\% \\
		Public transport & 35.1\% & [33.4\% ; 36.8\%] & 35.7\% \\
		Car              & 43.2\% & [41.2\% ; 45.4\%] & 42.5\% \\
	\end{tabular}
	\captionof{table}{Predicted versus actual weighted market shares\label{tab:actual}}
\end{table}

The values of our actual choices are close to the values predicted by our model ($\text{Model}_\text{pref}$): the differences are at most 0.7 percentage point. The model slightly overestimates the car share (43.2\% vs. 42.5\%) and slightly underestimates the public transport share (35.1\% vs. 35.7\%), while walking and cycling are almost exactly reproduced. For each mode, the actual value lies within the 90\% confidence interval. We can therefore validate $\text{Model}_\text{pref}$ as a good model to predict the market shares.
