\paragraph{Forecasting [6 points]} Consider the following scenarios: (i) an increase of car cost by 15\%; and (ii) a decrease of public transport cost by 15\%.

\begin{enumerate}
	\item Report the market shares predicted by $\text{Model}_\text{pref}$ for each scenario. Do they match your expectations? Compare the predicted market shares with the original market shares. \textbf{[1~point]}
\end{enumerate}

{\itshape
% % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % %
Each scenario is simulated with $\text{Model}_\text{pref}$ by multiplying the cost variable in the corresponding utility function: $1.15 \cdot \beta_{cost} \cdot cost_{car}$ in $V_{car}$ for scenario (i), and $0.85 \cdot \beta_{cost} \cdot cost_{pt}$ in $V_{pt}$ for scenario (ii). All other utilities are unchanged. The market shares are the weighted means of the individual probabilities (same strata weights as before), and the 90\% confidence intervals come from the same 100 bootstrap draws.

\begin{table}[h]
	\centering
	\small
	\begin{tabular}{l|c|cc|cc}
		Mode & Base & (i) Car +15\% & 90\% CI & (ii) PT $-15$\% & 90\% CI \\
		\hline
		Walking          & 18.8\% & 18.8\% & [17.6\% ; 20.0\%] & 18.7\% & [17.5\% ; 19.9\%] \\
		Cycling          & 2.9\%  & 2.9\%  & [2.4\% ; 3.5\%]   & 2.8\%  & [2.3\% ; 3.4\%] \\
		Public transport & 35.1\% & 35.5\% & [33.9\% ; 37.4\%] & 35.7\% & [34.1\% ; 37.5\%] \\
		Car              & 43.2\% & 42.8\% & [40.7\% ; 44.6\%] & 42.8\% & [40.7\% ; 44.6\%] \\
	\end{tabular}
	\captionof{table}{Weighted market shares predicted by $\text{Model}_\text{pref}$ for each scenario\label{tab:scenarios}}
\end{table}

The results match our expectations in terms of direction:
\begin{itemize}
	\item In scenario (i), the car becomes more expensive, so its share decreases (43.2\% $\rightarrow$ 42.8\%), and the lost users mainly shift to public transport (35.1\% $\rightarrow$ 35.5\%). Walking and cycling are almost unchanged, as they do not depend on cost.
	\item In scenario (ii), public transport becomes cheaper, so its share increases (35.1\% $\rightarrow$ 35.7\%). This gain comes from the car (43.2\% $\rightarrow$ 42.8\%) but also slightly from walking and cycling ($-0.1$ point each).
\end{itemize}
However, the changes are small (less than 1 percentage point) and the base shares are inside the 90\% confidence intervals of both scenarios. Our model is therefore very stable: a 15\% change in cost has only a limited impact on mode choice, because travel time (in logarithm), age and the traffic variability weigh much more in the utilities than cost ($\beta_{cost} = -0.123$).
% % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % %
\par}\medskip

\begin{enumerate}[resume]
	\item  Which scenario is the most effective policy if the goal is to decrease the share of car? Explain why. \textbf{[0.5~point]} 
\end{enumerate}

{\itshape
% % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % %
Both scenarios decrease the car share by almost the same amount: 43.22\% $\rightarrow$ 42.78\% ($-0.44$ point) in scenario (i) and 43.22\% $\rightarrow$ 42.76\% ($-0.46$ point) in scenario (ii). Scenario (ii) is therefore very slightly more effective, but the difference (0.02 point) is negligible compared with the confidence intervals, so the two policies can be considered equivalent for this goal.
The two policies differ however in their side effects. Scenario (ii) also attracts walkers and cyclists to public transport, and it strongly reduces the public transport revenue (question 3). Scenario (i) reduces the car share as much while increasing the public transport revenue. If the goal is only to decrease the car share, both are equivalent; if the cost of the policy is taken into account, scenario (i) is the most efficient.
