\begin{enumerate}[resume]
	\item Calculate the total public transportation revenue in each scenario. Which scenario reports the highest total revenue? Explain why. Is it higher than the total revenue obtained without any of the policies? Can you explain why? \textbf{[1.5~points]} 
\end{enumerate}

{\itshape
% % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % %
For each scenario, the revenue per person is the weighted average, over the sample, of the probability of choosing public transport multiplied by the public transport fare paid in the scenario (in euros, $\times 0.85$ in scenario (ii)). The total revenue is then obtained by scaling it to the whole London population ($N = 8'673'713$):
\[
R = N \cdot \frac{\sum_{n=1}^{S} \omega_{g(n)} \cdot P_n(pt) \cdot cost_{pt,n}}{\sum_{n=1}^{S} \omega_{g(n)}}
\]

\begin{table}[h]
	\centering
	\begin{tabular}{l|ccc}
		Scenario & Revenue per person (EUR) & Total revenue (EUR) & Change vs. base \\
		\hline
		Base (no policy)       & 0.81 & 7'063'759.87 & -- \\
		(i) Car cost +15\%     & 0.83 & 7'168'796.90 & +1.49\% \\
		(ii) PT cost $-15$\%   & 0.71 & 6'157'488.22 & $-12.83$\% \\
	\end{tabular}
	\captionof{table}{Total public transport revenue in each scenario\label{tab:revenue}}
\end{table}

\begin{itemize}
	\item \textbf{Scenario 1 (car cost +15\%) gives the highest public transport revenue.} Fares are unchanged, and the PT share rises from about 35.1\% to 35.5\%, roughly +1\%. Revenue therefore rises by about 1\% over the base.
	\item \textbf{Scenario 2 (PT cost -15\%) gives lower revenue than the base.} Fares fall by 15\%, and the PT share rises only about 1.7\% (35.1\% to 35.7\%). Revenue falls by about 13\%.
	\item \textbf{So scenario 1 is higher than the no-policy revenue, and scenario 2 is lower.} The reason is that demand for public transport is inelastic with respect to its own price (see question 5, where the own elasticity is about -0.1). When the fare falls, the lost revenue per trip outweighs the extra trips. When the car gets more expensive, extra people switch to PT at the same fare, which adds revenue with no price cut.
\end{itemize}
% % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % %
