\begin{enumerate}[resume]
	\item Calculate the average value of time for car and public transportation. Comment on the obtained results. \textbf{[1~point]}
\end{enumerate}

{\itshape
% % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % %
The value of time is the ratio between the derivative of the utility with respect to time and its derivative with respect to cost. As travel times enter $\text{Model}_\text{pref}$ in logarithm, the VOT is not constant but depends on the travel time of each individual:
\[
VOT_{n,i} = \frac{\partial V_i / \partial t_{in}}{\partial V_i / \partial c_{in}} = \frac{\beta_{time,i}}{\beta_{cost} \cdot t_{in}}
\]
It is simulated for each individual and then averaged with the strata weights.

\begin{table}[h]
	\centering
	\begin{tabular}{l|cccccc}
		Mode & Weighted mean & Mean (unweighted) & Median & 25\% & 75\% & Max \\
		\hline
		Car              & 91.6 & 92.3 & 65.3 & 34.6 & 118.3 & 1'189.5 \\
		Public transport & 49.0 & 49.3 & 35.0 & 21.1 & 61.5  & 1'820.3 \\
	\end{tabular}
	\captionof{table}{Value of time (EUR/h) for car and public transport\label{tab:vot}}
\end{table}

The average VOT is 91.6 EUR/h for car and 49.0 EUR/h for public transport: car users value their travel time almost twice as much as public transport users. A possible explanation is that time spent in public transport can be used for other activities (reading, working), whereas driving is more stressful and tiring. The weighting has only a small effect (92.3 $\rightarrow$ 91.6 and 49.3 $\rightarrow$ 49.0).

These averages are high because of the logarithmic specification: the VOT is inversely proportional to the travel time, so it becomes very large for very short trips. The distributions are therefore very skewed (maximum of 1'189.5 EUR/h for car and 1'820.3 EUR/h for public transport), and the mean is much higher than the median (65.3 and 35.0 EUR/h). The median is a more representative value of the VOT of a typical traveller.
