\begin{enumerate}[resume]
	\item Compute the direct and cross aggregate elasticities of car cost and public transport cost. Comment on the obtained results. Report the normalization factors. \textbf{[2~points]}
\end{enumerate}

{\itshape
% % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % % %
For a logit model, the individual point elasticities of $P_n(i)$ with respect to the cost $c$ are:
\[
E^{P_n(i)}_{c_{in}} = \beta_{cost} \cdot c_{in} \cdot \left(1 - P_n(i)\right) \qquad \text{(direct)}, \qquad
E^{P_n(i)}_{c_{jn}} = -\beta_{cost} \cdot c_{jn} \cdot P_n(j) \qquad 
\]
The aggregate elasticity is the weighted sum of the individual elasticities, divided by the weighted number of people choosing mode $i$ (normalization factor):
\[
E^{W(i)}_{c} = \frac{\sum_{n=1}^{S} \omega_{g(n)} \, P_n(i) \, E^{P_n(i)}_{c}}{\sum_{n=1}^{S} \omega_{g(n)} \, P_n(i)}
\]

\begin{table}[h]
	\centering
	\begin{tabular}{l|l|c|c}
		Share of & With respect to & Type & Aggregate elasticity \\
		\hline
		Car              & Car cost & direct & $-0.071$ \\
		Car              & PT cost  & cross  & $+0.071$ \\
		Public transport & PT cost  & direct & $-0.108$ \\
		Public transport & Car cost & cross  & $+0.075$ \\
	\end{tabular}
	\captionof{table}{Aggregate direct and cross elasticities with respect to cost\label{tab:elast}}
\end{table}

\begin{table}[h]
	\centering
	\begin{tabular}{l|c}
		Normalization factor & Value \\
		\hline
		Sum of the weights (sample size), $\sum_n \omega_{g(n)}$ & 5'000.0 \\
		Weighted number choosing car, $\sum_n \omega_{g(n)} P_n(car)$ & 2'161.0 \\
		Weighted number choosing PT, $\sum_n \omega_{g(n)} P_n(pt)$ & 1'757.4 \\
	\end{tabular}
	\captionof{table}{Normalization factors\label{tab:norm}}
\end{table}

\begin{itemize}
	\item Direct car elasticity is about -0.06, cross elasticity of car share with respect to PT cost about +0.06, direct PT elasticity about -0.11, and cross PT elasticity with respect to car cost about +0.08.
	\item All four are below 1 in absolute value, so demand for both modes is inelastic. Public transport is more responsive to its own cost than car demand is, but the effect is still small.
	\item The cross elasticities are positive, which confirms that car and PT are substitutes: a higher cost for one mode pushes some people to the other. The effects are modest because the cost coefficient is small (about -0.12 per euro), so cost changes move choices only slightly.
	\item The normalisation factors are the sum of the weights (5,000), and the weighted numbers choosing car and PT, sum(w * P). Each aggregate elasticity divides the weighted sum of individual elasticities by the weighted number of people choosing that mode.
\end{itemize}
